WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Film Courage: How do you keep faith in yourself&nbsp;
and in your craft during turbulent times?

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.320
Brad Rushing, Cinematographer: Boy is that&nbsp;
a good question! Talk about dirty laundry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.200
I have this terrific career coach. Her name&nbsp;
is Erica Wernick and she’s really fabulous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.880
You should interview her. She’s got a book&nbsp;
coming out. I’ll send you her information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.520
But she would probably punch me&nbsp;
in the arm for saying this, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:35.120
there was a time early in my career (early on&nbsp;
in the 90’s) where I was so frustrated and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.120
down and didn’t believe in myself and I just had&nbsp;
this revelation You know what, I’ve set a goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:49.280
I don’t have to believe in myself. I just have&nbsp;
to do the steps that I committed to doing to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:56.320
me through the valley of the shadow of death and&nbsp;
that worked for me. It was a device that worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:03.120
for me and I feel like occasionally I still have&nbsp;
it. I mean I think a lot of people like Erica talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.400
about how important it is to believe in yourself&nbsp;
and to believe that your success is inevitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:15.680
And that is also very powerful. I just find that&nbsp;
my brain is a little more complex in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.840
the things it does to me. I don’t know&nbsp;
that I am always in the driver’s seat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:30.480
and quite honestly I deal with self-esteem issues.&nbsp;
I really do. I think a lot of people do in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.920
business. Your identity is tied up in your work&nbsp;
even if you don’t want it to be because you put so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.240
much of yourself into it and if it doesn’t do well&nbsp;
or even if it does well I mean honestly the irony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.400
to me of having something successful and praised&nbsp;
and then not having a lot of work, that’s even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.760
worse than having something criticized because&nbsp;
if least if they criticized it you are like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:00.000
okay it’s garbage, I get it. But if they say it’s&nbsp;
awesome and then the jobs don’t follow you’re like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.280
where’s the karma, where’s the payoff,&nbsp;
we made a deal. So that is harder for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:22.640
I do try and keep faith in myself and you know&nbsp;
honestly when I do have crisis with the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:31.120
or success is a nebulous thing I really get basic&nbsp;
and I go back and remind you know Brad you're an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.960
artist. That was the contract you made. You never&nbsp;
said you wanted to be rich. I don’t care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.440
being rich. I would like to be self-sufficient.&nbsp;
I’d like to be secure. If I was rich it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:51.200
okay but I’d be donating, helping, and I mean&nbsp;
I just don’t need a super yacht and an island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:59.440
But I remind myself of the contract that I made&nbsp;
was that I wanted to be an artist, simple as that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:08.000
I look at the work that I’ve done, I look&nbsp;
at the work that I am doing and even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.960
those people don’t like it or those people don't&nbsp;
like it or even if everybody likes it and I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.600
get a job or an award, it’s like you know what?&nbsp;
You’ve got to do the work. Somebody gave you the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.520
toys. Somebody gave you the sandbox and they let&nbsp;
you build your sandcastle and then that’s enough.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.840
Film Courage: Do you think a lot of success&nbsp;
is luck or success can be orchestrated?

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.280
Brad: I want to believe it&nbsp;
can be orchestrated and I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.000
people who feel very strongly&nbsp;
about that and I feel…I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.600
feel it can be influenced. I don’t know if I&nbsp;
believe it can be orchestrated in a guaranteed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:59.307
way because for a hundred people doing a&nbsp;
hundred things more or less the same they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.307 --> 00:04:03.920
are going to have different outcomes because&nbsp;
they are going to know different people. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:10.160
will be at different moments in time. It will be&nbsp;
a different project. They may have a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:16.640
mode to their personality or their energy.&nbsp;
I don’t know? It can be simple things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.080
I’ll give you an example. I have people who&nbsp;
message me out of the blue and if people message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.640
me and they have a specific question and they say&nbsp;
Brad what light did you use in that particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:37.360
scene or they say would you look at my resume, I&nbsp;
respond to them. People who hit me up and they say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.200
How are you doing? I just don’t even answer&nbsp;
because how do I…I don’t need any small talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:52.720
and how do I answer that? So the point being&nbsp;
is that the way you interact with the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:01.440
be it me or be it those persons emailing me you’re&nbsp;
going to get a different response depending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.480
what you put out there and I've forgotten&nbsp;
your question but I hope you answered it.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.920
Film Courage: Oh you did, you did. Because I&nbsp;
was saying is success - is a lot of it luck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.080
or can it be orchestrated so it sounds&nbsp;
like it might be sort of a combination?

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:24.240
Brad: It’s got to be, it really has to&nbsp;
be because what is it they say? Success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:32.400
is preparation meeting opportunity right? You&nbsp;
have to be prepared. If you go into that meeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.080
if you luck out and you get that meeting but you&nbsp;
don’t know squat you’re not going to impress them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.064
But if you are a genius and you’re an introvert&nbsp;
and won’t go out and do the meetings, then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.064 --> 00:05:50.000
are still stuck and just for anybody who is an&nbsp;
introvert I am a terrible introvert. I am so shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:57.360
but I want this more than I want to be shy so&nbsp;
I just…I’m not an actor but in that way I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:04.480
an actor. I will go do the act and I’m not always&nbsp;
good. If I’m in a crowd and I don’t know anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.160
and I don’t have a wingman, that’s kryptonite. I&nbsp;
shut down and I sit in the corner to this day I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:17.680
ashamed of it but I’m powerless. But if I have a&nbsp;
wingman, if I don’t have somebody else to help me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:25.920
or at least find “Oh, I know that person!”&nbsp;
They can introduce me. When I was a little kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:35.200
my mom would say do you want a slurpee? And I’d&nbsp;
be sitting in the car and she’s say you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.320
go in and get it I would just sit there&nbsp;
and cry because I didn’t want to go in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.200
by myself I was so shy, so&nbsp;
it was hard you know. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.360
if you want something enough then you do the&nbsp;
things you need to do. One of my favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.080
sayings right now because I’m looking to level&nbsp;
up. Look I mean I could be at the top and I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.400
still be looking to level up but there’s this&nbsp;
great but there’s this great saying and it’s if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.160
you want something you’ve never had before you&nbsp;
have to do something you’ve never done before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.760
and sometimes that might be obvious but sometimes&nbsp;
it’s surprising. Just the other day I had this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:22.000
realization because I’m a workaholic and I drive&nbsp;
myself like crazy and I was reading Erica’s book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.416
as a matter of fact [Meant For This: The Mindset&nbsp;
And Strategy To Achieve Your Most "Impossible"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.416 --> 00:07:26.720
Dreams by Erica Wernick]. Meant For This -&nbsp;
go out and buy it. It’s a really good book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.480
but I had a thought that was really kind of&nbsp;
a non-sequitur. It wasn’t really relevant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:39.920
what I was reading but I thought Brad you&nbsp;
need quiet time. You need to stop and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:45.600
be quiet whether you meditate or just watch&nbsp;
the world go by because you don't do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.160
and that's surprising because my logical brain&nbsp;
said well that’s not going to get you anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.400
But then I start thinking because I know that&nbsp;
you just can’t go 24 hours even though I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:06.800
because you will wear down. You will lose your&nbsp;
acuity so maybe that is the thing? Maybe I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:16.240
more breaks and more quiet time because maybe that&nbsp;
will make the time I do spend with working just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:24.480
that more focused and that more effective.&nbsp;
It’s better to spend five really effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:33.120
minutes than an hour kind of unfocused, tired and&nbsp;
distracted. So that was the thing that I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.480
that's interesting because that’s a thing I hadn’t&nbsp;
done before and it's not what I would have guessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:50.000
but you know that’s just kind of the thing about&nbsp;
success is when people ask advice about success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.480
or about being a cinematographer or being&nbsp;
on set or meeting people or networking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.160
everybody wants the formula, they want the&nbsp;
roadmap. They want the A-B-C equals D and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.520
I get that because as humans I think we're wired&nbsp;
that way. I want that but guess what? I’ve never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:14.320
found it. It’s just always different. It’s a&nbsp;
moving target and you learn as you go and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.240
take your best guess and you try it and&nbsp;
if it doesn’t work then you’re like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.960
it’s not working, I’m not going to quit but let me&nbsp;
try something different. Let me revise it. Can I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.480
go this way? Can I go that way? Can I show up with&nbsp;
flowers and chocolate? Can I get my buddy to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:38.880
an introduction? Whatever you know. It’s not about&nbsp;
stopping it's about okay this isn’t working, let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:44.720
think of something else. Let’s reinvent and&nbsp;
even something that does work for awhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.720
just like the market changes, just like the&nbsp;
technology changes it’s like you know what?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.040
It used to work, it’s not working&nbsp;
now, try something different.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:59.840
Film Courage: So being internal (an introvert),&nbsp;
maybe needing quiet time, did that work for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:06.880
growing up in Houston? How did you turn that…like&nbsp;
if you needed to turn on…and I’m just stereotyping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.760
here…but the Southern charm and being sort of&nbsp;
larger than life, which you do have that side to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.840
you. I can see it, but how would you manifest that&nbsp;
if you had to walk into a room and sort of own it?

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:26.720
Brad: If I know people, it’s easy.&nbsp;
If I know some people, it’s easy. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:31.840
part of it is having generosity&nbsp;
towards the other person. I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.080
one of the things that I tell young people is&nbsp;
if you approach a relationship, if you want a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:46.080
meeting or you want to connect with somebody,&nbsp;
do not approach it from what can they do for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:52.880
I am going to ask them for a job. I am going&nbsp;
to ask them to give me something. The best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:11:02.480
way to approach it is to be like what can I give&nbsp;
them. What do I have to offer and you don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:08.480
to be an experienced filmmaker. We are blessed&nbsp;
with social media where we consider this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.840
likes going out on a boat, this person&nbsp;
likes dogs, and that person likes to paint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:21.440
and this person maybe went to your school and&nbsp;
this person is maybe friends with your uncle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:28.080
I don’t know? But we have the ability to do&nbsp;
due diligence on people in a way that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:35.840
just never been possible before. I do that and I&nbsp;
recommend that people do that and you go and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:41.440
do your homework and then they’re not a stranger&nbsp;
you know because you find that think Ohhh! The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:48.480
like comic books or their favorite movie is my&nbsp;
favorite movie or they like the same band I like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:57.600
That takes a lot of the edge off of because the&nbsp;
part of the fear is like I don’t know what to say?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:05.040
But if you guys both like Doberman Pinschers,&nbsp;
now you have something to talk about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.360
And it’s lovely for the other person&nbsp;
because you know what all day long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.280
they are getting calls oh give me a job.&nbsp;
I want to be a PA, will you make my movie?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:17.360
And they are like Oh? You want to talk about&nbsp;
my dog? Here’s a picture of my dog. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:23.360
so happy. And that’s the thing because you have&nbsp;
to remember this is a business of relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:32.080
It’s not a business of parasites and prey&nbsp;
you know? It’s your friends, you want to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.440
with your friends. You’re going to call your&nbsp;
friends, you want to work with your friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:40.400
Everybody likes hanging out with their friends and&nbsp;
this is a business where you have the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:49.360
hire the people you like. It’s just like in your&nbsp;
life. Think about that one friend who always asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:54.640
you for something. They always want you to come&nbsp;
over to their house and do them a favor and buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.640 --> 00:13:00.720
lunch and they never reciprocate. How excited&nbsp;
are you to spend time with that person? But think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:04.560
about the other person who calls you up and says&nbsp;
Oh, there’s this new movie and you would LOVE it!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:11.360
That’s the person you’re happy to hear from.&nbsp;
So when I meet with people I try to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.600
to offer that and also when I follow up and&nbsp;
in social media I’m like Oh hey, here’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:22.080
article I think you’ll like about butterflies,&nbsp;
here’s a butterfly article. And it’s not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:31.280
a cynical thing because I think it’s cool that&nbsp;
they like butterflies. That’s cool. So when I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:40.240
in a position of meeting people one of the things&nbsp;
that that mindset precipitates is that it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:44.960
you listen because you don't know what people&nbsp;
want if you don't listen to them and a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.600
people will tell you that in meetings you don’t&nbsp;
want to do a lot of talking. You want to listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:57.200
and then talk strategically and to the point.&nbsp;
And so I think if you listen people are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.000
to tell you their story and they’re going to&nbsp;
tell you what they need. And I’ve had that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:05.920
times where they are like Oh I was on this film&nbsp;
shoot and there was this thing and I’m like oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:11.280
yeah. Well the next time you have that happen let&nbsp;
me know because I know a guy. Or oh well let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:18.480
tell you what I did once in a similar situation&nbsp;
and I think that’s again you're being friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:27.840
I literally years ago stopped leading with my&nbsp;
website. Like the most aggressive thing I will do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:34.160
now is put it in my signature because if people&nbsp;
are interested they’re going to look for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:41.360
I reach out and talk to them about their Doberman&nbsp;
Pinscher or their horse or their butterflies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:46.560
I mean that's how I build it or I compliment&nbsp;
them like Oh that commercial you did was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:55.760
beautiful or or hey I am also friends with so and&nbsp;
so. I've done lunches with people and we spend…and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:01.600
this is somebody that yeah I’d love to work with&nbsp;
them and we spend the whole lunch talking nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:09.360
having remotely to do with film and as we’re&nbsp;
getting up to leave they say “Send me your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:16.960
work. I might have something coming up.” But how&nbsp;
much more powerful is it for them to ask then for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:23.040
me to be like Ohhh! Look at my stuff! Because&nbsp;
they get that all day long and again you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:27.360
don't want to be that person. I don’t want…maybe&nbsp;
somebody does but I don't want to be that person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:33.200
So that is how I handle it and also it takes&nbsp;
the pressure off. I have nothing to prove,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:39.120
I’m not selling anything, I’m not a salesman.&nbsp;
I would be a horrible salesman. I’m pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:43.680
at being nice to people. I’m pretty good…I mean&nbsp;
something I care a lot about is other people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:50.160
How can I help them? And again, maybe it's&nbsp;
not even me maybe it’s like you should meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:56.080
this person. I’m really good about that just be&nbsp;
a matchmaker of these two people need to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:00.240
and sometimes it's obvious why they should meet&nbsp;
and sometimes it's just like y’all have similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.960
personalities and I don't know what’s going to&nbsp;
come of it but you should know each other so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:12.080
that's my answer to your question as a shy person&nbsp;
you know how do you go in there. I go in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:21.840
with a sense of generosity and relating&nbsp;
as a human as opposed to a salesperson.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:31.680
Question For The Viewers: What was&nbsp;
the best part of this video for you?

